        "title": "Saussure: Sign, Signifier, Signified",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Ferdinand de Saussure (1857–1913) is the founding figure of modern linguistics and, through his influence on structuralism, of semiotics broadly. His Cours de linguistique générale (Course in General Linguistics, 1916) was published posthumously from student notes — he never published it himself. Despite this, it became one of the most influential texts of the 20th century, launching structuralism in linguistics, anthropology, literary theory, and philosophy."
    },
    {
      "id": "01-SAUSSURE.md#003",
      "path": [
        "01-SAUSSURE.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:semiotics:01-saussure, git-history:semiotics:01-saussure]",
